---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const entries = [

{
    date: '2026-10-14',
    title: 'Used Xvfb for GMAT screenshot within subtask and adjusted some settings within the orbital view of GMAT.',
    summary: 'Tasks were labeled TJC-3: Capture a real GMAT GUI screenshot using Xvfb and fix missing OrbitView textures/models, TJC-1.16: Harden the complete workflow after the early vertical slice, TJC-4 - Refresh the committed GMAT vertical-slice example with a real GMAT GUI screenshot and TJC-1.8: Add focused tests for the scoped end-to-end workflow. Explanations below.'
    details: 'Subagents ran the first three tasks to successfully run GMAT and show evidence with a screenshot. The fourth subtask was an inspection of currently all 116 tests, which addressed a gap for GMAT execution/CW singularity issue and another gap to ensure all skills point to the source code for any numerical calculations.'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-12',
    title: 'Iterated on GMAT use and ',
    summary: 'Tasks were labeled TJC-2: Capture a real GMAT GUI screenshot and store it in a discoverable location (failed), TJC-1.19: Return a unified numerical and visual verification report and TJC-1.9: Create the four-category rendezvous benchmark. Explanations below.'
    details: 'Subagents ran the first two tasks to attempt screenshotting again. After both failed at the screenshot task, main agent recommended downloading Xvfb for screenshot capabilities for the agent--so I downloaded that. The third task created a benchmark of the example problems I wanted to initially solve for using the Copilot.'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-11',
    title: 'Added a model selector skill and attempted GMAT screenshot.',
    summary: 'Tasks were labeled TJC-1.14: Implement assumption-based CW or two-body model selection and TJC-1.21: Harden GMAT execution, comparison, and screenshot capture. Explanations below.'
    details: 'Subagents ran 1.14 to create a skill that allows the problem formalizer to hand off information to another skill that will select the correct model for implementation (currently only CW and 2-body models available for scope). The hardening of GMAT did not produce a correct screenshot, but rather an SVG 2-D image that was accurate but not really helpful for any mission planning.'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-09',
    title: 'Addressed more test cases with subtasks from backlog and started first attempt at utilizing GMAT for verification with user.',
    summary: 'Tasks were labeled TJC-1.6: Verify LVLH/inertial state transformations, TJC-1.12: Narrow and harden rendezvous problem formalizer, TJC-1.2: Align controlled reference and scoped skills and TJC-1.20: Build early CW-to-GMAT vertical slice. Explanations below.'
    details: 'Subagents did two tasks running extra test cases for the frame translation (from LVLH for CW to inertial for two-body)', and narrowed the scope for what the Copilot will actually solve given specific inputs. Also, a subagent decided to split up my two skills into four here (TJC 1.2), but I'm not sure this is the best way to continue and may have it revert back if it becomes too complicated. The first integration of GMAT took a while, but opened up the actual GUI multiple times which could be useful in the future to show the user/mission designer.'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-08',
    title: 'Resolved singularity issue within code base using State Transition Matrices with backlog tasks and ',
    summary: 'Subagents completed tasks to not only identify the singularity issue, but solve for an answer when a case that results in matrix singularity is presented.',
    details: 'Tasks were labeled TJC-1.3: Audit CW state-transition implementation, TJC-1.4: Handle singular CW two-burn targeting and TJC-1.15: Harden deterministic CW rendezvous solution path'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-06',
    title: 'Addressed two tasks next to-do',
    summary: 'Had a subagent complete TJC 1.5 — Harden the deterministic two-body rendezvous fallback and two tasks for verification with Poliastro.',
    details: 'The hardening task and poliastro tasks ran in parallel and had two separate goals--the hardening task set up tests for correct units and frames within the problem, while TJC-1.7: Verify two-body propagation against pinned Poliastro and TJC-1.27: Define pinned Poliastro verification architecture were tasks that allowed for integration with the Poliastro astrodynamics github library. This will most likely need to be refined in future iterations since I will have my agent working in a sandbox--right now it access poliastro documentation/orbital equations via the web.'
},

{
    date: '2026-10-01',
    title: 'Broke project up into tasks with backlog',
    summary: 'I gave a project scope within a pdf to my pi harness and had it create agent-level tasks for my current semester project.',
    details: 'The initial project included 23 sub-tasks to Task TJC 1 (TraJectory Copilot 1 main task). I then asked within the same session to adjust the Defintion of Done paramaters to include updating my educational/walkthrough site and usng atomic commits to git for traceability. My current project was in another folder, so one of the first tasks was to copy over everything I had begun to implement. I also realzied I wanted another verification step with Poliastro (which is the last task add listed here) after talking wiht my lab mentor. I ran tasks: TJC-1.1 — Recovered baseline implementation; TJC-1.24 — Synchronized upstream workspace and educational site; TJC-1.25 — Aligned site with scoped roadmap and living progress; TJC-1.26 — Established initial checkpoint and atomic commit policy; TJC-1.27 — Defined pinned Poliastro verification architecture.'
},

{
    date: '2026-09-25',
    title: 'Reproduced a GUI for space operators from a masters thesis.',
    summary: 'After re-creating the graphs below, I thought it would be interesting to have my agent create a GUI like the one described in an AFIT (Air Force Institute of Technology) masters thesis that evaluated the best user interface for space operators to achieve their goals on-console and when conducting operations.',
    details: `After a couple of iterations, it created a cool example that is a nice superficial GUI that maps out what would be necessary/helpful for operators on console. It missed a couple key aspects from the user interface in the thesis, but it did make the interface look very nice. You can also view the public repository here: <a href="https://github.com/annarich00/EID_UI" class="underline text-orbital" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">eid-ui</a>. This is a Flask app.`
  },

{
    date: '2026-09-24',
    title: 'Reproduced two graphs within orbital dynamics field.',
    summary: 'I first had pi (not using nono sandbox) re-create a graph displaying the necessary altitude vs inclination to keep a repeating ground track. I thought this was a good, simple task to start with and it performed extremely well. After this, I had it overlay its results with the papers graph--which proved successful. The second graph I had it create was a pareto solution set for delta v and time within the classic Clohessy-Wiltshire problem. There was no graph for it to reproduce, but I had an expected curve for it to find--which it did very well.',
    details: `My prompt for the first graph reproduction is here: "Reproduce figure 4 in this paper accurately: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/340449269_RECONFIGURABLE_SATELLITE_CONSTELLATIONS_FOR_GEO-SPATIALLY_ADAPTIVE_EARTH_OBSERVATION_MISSIONS. Use uv for project management and dependency management. Follow test-driven development (TDD). Build a minimal implementation first. Create property-based tests using pytest and hypothesis whenever appropriate. Prefer physical invariants and orbital-mechanics consistency checks over implementation-specific tests." The graphs below show the three outputs I described in the summary.`
  },

{
    date: '2026-09-22',
    title: 'Tested nono within pi for restrictions.',
    summary: 'I asked pi with nono sandbox to find Dr. Richard Linares most recent paper on Large Language Models in astrodynamics and summarize it, attempting to hit a sandbox error.',
    details: `The model thought for a long time, giving many answers like: "I'm running inside a nono sandbox that only allows network access to parley.api.mit.edu. I don't have direct internet access to arxiv.org or general web search. Let me check what capabilities the parley proxy offers — it might provide search/fetch tools." It also ran its own version of nono why, recieving an unsatisfactory answer (appararently) since it kept trying. It came to the conclusion it only has access to the website Parley and nothing else. It then asked if it could have more permissions when I exited the session which I found interesting.`
  },

{
    date: '2026-09-17',
    title: 'Added my first skill: orbital-problem-formalizer',
    summary: "This skill formats a problem into specific orbital dynamics requirements and evaluates if it is ready to solve, missing key information or has an error in how it could be implemented.",
    details: "The idea behind this skill is to first format a problem into something that can be handed off to another skill that has the orbital dynamics math in it. I ran three tests that I placed in a folder called 'evaluations' and also wrote my findings in my file baseline_comparison.md within my pi/trajectory-copilot folder. the first and last problem i gave the LLM gave my expected output, but the second gave a 'partial pass' according to my expectations. the LLM interprets the skill very conservatively, which may have to be adjusted in the future if it cannot reason through certain problems."
  },

  {
    date: '2026-09-24',
    title: 'Counted my lengthiest session on September 11 by session messages and tool calls',
    summary: "Messages: 58 Tool Calls: 174 ",
    details: "I used these two commands to investigate the details of my run on Sept 11: grep -o ''role':'user'' ~/.pi/agent/sessions/--home-anna_ford--/2026-09-11T18-38-51-749Z_01a091c3-ed62-701b-81b5-ecbd1ab73f3c.jsonl | wc -l and grep -o ''type':'toolCall'' ~/.pi/agent/sessions/--home-anna_ford--/2026-09-11T18-38-51-749Z_01a091c3-ed62-701b-81b5-ecbd1ab73f3c.jsonl | wc -l"
  },

  {
    date: '2026-09-14',
    title: 'Added a click feature',
    summary: "I wanted to add a click feature to take me to a site where I can present weekly to my advisor with progess I've made and updates.",
    details: "Clicking \"Ctrl+S\" opens up a \"thesis\" pop-up that should have a link to the site in my own repository, where I update my weekly progress with papers I've read pertaining to my research."
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-11',
    title: 'Started this Assignment',
    summary: "I gave a quick prompt to GPT mini 5.4, requesting a website meeting the class requirements. I refined the website based on other students' projects and my expectations.",
    details: "The resulting product was a very basic HTML site. When I saw other students work, I asked the model to refine what I had originally asked for and to use the tools they used, Astro and Tailwind CSS."
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Dev Log | Agents in a Rendezvous" description="Weekly research updates and progress notes.">
  <section class="space-y-6">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Dev Log</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-300">These entries summarize the website content drafted during this session. Add future updates only when new site content or project progress is ready.</p>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      {entries.map((entry) => (
        <details class="card group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
            <p class="text-sm text-orbital">{entry.date}</p>
            <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold">{entry.title}</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-slate-300">{entry.summary}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-thruster">Click to expand for more detail.</p>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 border-t border-white/10 pt-4 text-slate-300">
            <div set:html={entry.details} />
            {entry.date === '2026-09-25' && entry.title === 'Reproduced a GUI for space operators from a masters thesis.' && (
              <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
                <figure>
                  <figcaption class="mb-2 text-center text-sm font-medium text-slate-200">eid-ui SS of home page.png</figcaption>
                  <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}dev-log/eid-ui-homepage.png`} alt="EID UI home page screenshot" class="w-full rounded-xl border border-white/10" />
                </figure>
              </div>
            )}

          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Template for future entries</h2>
      <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-black/30 p-4 text-sm text-slate-200">Date: YYYY-MM-DD
Title: Short update
Summary: One-sentence overview of the new site or project work.
Details: Expanded details on the specific changes made.</pre>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
